inal recorded';\n    }\n  }\n  infoBar.classList.toggle('has-proxy', on);\n  infoBar.classList.toggle('has-orig', showing);\n}\n\n// v7.28.0: which file a proxy card stands for, and the bytes to prove it.\n//\n// `_dropFiles` is the whole trick. When a proxy and its original arrive in the\n// same drag, the ORIGINAL is paired away and never becomes a card \u2014 but we\n// already hold its File. Remembering it by name means \"Link original\" is\n// instant instead of sending the user back to a file picker for a file they\n// literally just dragged in.\nconst _dropFiles = new Map();          // drop key  -> File (video only); see dropKeyOf\nconst _DROP_FILES_MAX = 600;           // v7.30.1: FIFO cap, see noteDroppedFiles\nconst proxyOriginals = new Map();      // item id   -> { name, url, file }\n\n// v7.30.0: one definition of \"which entry does this file live under\".\n// A bare NAME is not an identity either: A/shot01.mov and B/shot01.mov are\n// two different takes, and keying by name alone means the second one\n// overwrites the first, so the first card can no longer find its own\n// original and gets sent to a picker for a file it already dragged in.\n// v7.30.1: on a DROP Chrome leaves `webkitRelativePath` EMPTY -- that field\n// is only ever filled for a `webkitdirectory` input. Krafted's own folder\n// reader writes `_kraftedPath` instead (see readEntry), so that is what has\n// to be read first, and `webkitRelativePath` is only a fallback. With no path\n// at all -- a plain multi-select -- the name is the best we have, and then\n// `originalFileFor` insists the size agrees before it will use it.\nfunction dropKeyOf(f) {\n  if (!f) return '';\n  const name = String(f.name || '').trim();\n  // v7.30.1: `_kraftedPath` is the FOLDER, not the file -- readEntry stamps\n  // the containing directory. So the name has to be appended here, or every\n  // file in one folder collapses into a single pool entry.\n  const dir = (typeof f._kraftedPath === 'string') ? f._kraftedPath.trim().replace(/\\/+$/, '') : '';\n  if (dir) return dir + '/' + name;\n  const rel = (typeof f.webkitRelativePath === 'string') ? f.webkitRelativePath.trim() : '';\n  return rel || name;\n}\n\n// v7.29.0: video only, and bounded.\n// * video only -- the proxy workflow never has an image or a WAV standing in\n//   for anything, so remembering those would pin a whole PSD or a 300MB WAV\n//   in the renderer heap for the life of the session and buy nothing.\n// * bounded -- Map keeps insertion order, so evicting the oldest is a\n//   one-liner. v7.29.1: 600, not 200. A board holds ~300 items, so 300\n//   proxies plus their 300 originals is one ordinary drag, and at 200 the\n//   first-dropped originals were evicted before their own cards could read\n//   their size -- the user had dragged the file in and was still sent to a\n//   picker for it. The cap is generous because holding a File is cheap: a\n//   File is a REFERENCE to a file on disk, not its bytes. The memory that\n//   matters is the object URL, and this pool never creates one --\n//   applyOriginalLink does, and only when a card is actually linked.\nfunction noteDroppedFiles(files) {\n  (files || []).forEach(function (f) {\n    if (!f || !f.name) return;\n    if (!(f.type && f.type.indexOf('video/') === 0)) return;\n    const key = dropKeyOf(f);\n    if (!key) return;\n    if (_dropFiles.has(key)) _dropFiles.delete(key);  // re-drop: newest wins, and moves to the back\n    _dropFiles.set(key, f);\n    while (_dropFiles.size > _DROP_FILES_MAX) {\n      _dropFiles.delete(_dropFiles.keys().next().value);\n    }\n  });\n}\n\n// v7.29.0: the only way out of proxyOriginals.\n// Deleting the Map entry on its own is NOT enough -- the entry owns a blob\n// URL, and an un-revoked object URL pins its Blob for the life of the\n// document. Returns true when there was something to drop.\nfunction dropOriginalLink(itemId) {\n  const link = proxyOriginals.get(itemId);\n  if (!link) return false;\n  proxyOriginals.delete(itemId);\n  if (link.url) URL.revokeObjectURL(link.url);\n  return true;\n}\n\nfunction applyOriginalLink(item, file) {\n  if (!item || !file) return false;\n  dropOriginalLink(item.id);   // v7.30.1: linking twice must not leak the first URL\n  const url = URL.createObjectURL(file);\n  proxyOriginals.set(item.id, { name: file.name, url: url, file: file });\n  if (item.el) syncProxyBadgeEl(item.el.querySelector('.media-info-bar'), item);\n  return true;\n}\n\n// One definition of \"get me the original for this card\": use the file from the\n// same drop when we still have it, otherwise ask.\n//\n// v7.29.0: a name is not an identity. Two takes can both be called\n// \"shot01.mov\", and silently linking the wrong one is a conform that ships\n// the wrong footage -- far worse than one extra click. So the remembered\n// File also has to agree on size, and a card that never recorded a size\n// (restored from a save, or opened from a .kpak) goes to the picker instead\n// of guessing.\n// v7.29.1: one definition of \"do we already hold this card's original?\".\n// The batch action needs it up front to PLAN (how many of these will open a\n// dialog decides both the progress line and whether a cancel stops the batch)\n// and the single-card action needs it to link. A second copy of this test is\n// exactly how a card ends up with the wrong file.\n// v7.30.0: a card remembers WHERE its original came from, not just what it\n// is called -- that is what lets two takes with the same name both be found.\n// Session-only on purpose, like `originalSize`: the pool it unlocks dies with\n// the tab anyway, so writing it into a save would be a field that survives\n// reload and then proves nothing.\nfunction originalKeyFor(item) {\n  if (!item) return '';\n  const p = (typeof item.originalPath === 'string') ? item.originalPath.trim() : '';\n  return p || String(item.originalName || '').trim();\n}\n\n// One definition of \"does this File stand for this card?\", over any pool.\n// `needSize`: a size we recorded is a promise about WHICH take this is, so\n// when we know it, a file that disagrees is NOT this card's original. When we\n// do not know it there is nothing to disagree with -- and whether that is\n// acceptable depends on who handed us the file:\n//   * the drop pool says NO (v7.29.0: a guess is how a conform ships the\n//     wrong footage, and one extra dialog is cheap next to that);\n//   * files the user just picked themselves say YES -- they were pointing at\n//     these on purpose, and asking twice is worse than a name match.\nfunction originalFileFor(item, pool, needSize) {\n  if (!item || !pool) return null;\n  const key = originalKeyFor(item);\n  if (!key) return null;\n  const remembered = pool.get(key);\n  if (!remembered) return null;\n  const want = (typeof item.originalSize === 'number' && item.originalSize > 0) ? item.originalSize : 0;\n  if (!want) return needSize ? null : remembered;\n  return (remembered.size === want) ? remembered : null;\n}\n\nfunction rememberedOriginalFor(item) {\n  return originalFileFor(item, _dropFiles, true);\n}\n\nfunction linkOriginalForItem(item) {\n  if (!item || !item.isProxy) return Promise.resolve(false);\n  const already = rememberedOriginalFor(item);\n  if (already) {\n    applyOriginalLink(item, already);\n    return Promise.resolve(true);\n  }\n  return pickSingleFile('video/*').then(function (f) {\n    if (!f) return false;\n    return applyOriginalLink(item, f);\n  });\n}\n\n// A bare <input type=file> on purpose: it works in every browser, and since\n// the link is session-only there is nothing for a FileSystemFileHandle to\n// buy us yet.\n//\n// v7.29.0: ONE input, reused. Building one per call leaked it every time the\n// user cancelled the dialog -- `change` never fires, so the `inp.remove()`\n// that lived inside it never ran either. Two consequences of reusing it:\n//   * `value` has to be cleared before every click. A file input does not\n//     fire `change` when the same file is chosen again, so without this the\n//     second \"Link original\" of the same file reads as a dead menu entry.\n//   * `cancel` has to resolve. Escaping the dialog used to leave the promise\n//     pending forever, which is the same silence under a different name.\n//     (`cancel` is Chrome 113+; where it is missing we are no worse off than\n//     before, which is why there is no fallback hugging a timer.)\nlet _filePicker = null;\n// v7.30.0: ONE input serves both modes, and `multiple` is the only\n// difference. Everything else -- clear `value` before every click, resolve on\n// `cancel` -- is what keeps a REUSED input honest, and it has to hold in both\n// modes or the batch path inherits a bug the single path already fixed.\nfunction pickFiles(accept, multi) {\n  return new Promise(function (resolve) {\n    if (!_filePicker) {\n      _filePicker = document.createElement('input');\n      _filePicker.type = 'file';\n      _filePicker.style.display = 'none';\n      document.body.appendChild(_filePicker);\n    }\n    const inp = _filePicker;\n    inp.accept = accept || '';\n    inp.multiple = !!multi;\n    inp.value = '';\n    let settled = false;\n    function done(files) {\n      if (settled) return;\n      settled = true;\n      inp.removeEventListener('change', onChange);\n      inp.removeEventListener('cancel', onCancel);\n      resolve(files);\n    }\n    function onChange() {\n      const list = inp.files;\n      const out = [];\n      for (let i = 0; i < (list ? list.length : 0); i++) out.push(list[i]);\n      done(out.length ? out : null);\n    }\n    function onCancel() { done(null); }\n    inp.addEventListener('change', onChange);\n    inp.addEventListener('cancel', onCancel);\n    inp.click();\n  });\n}\n\nfunction pickSingleFile(accept) {\n  return pickFiles(accept, false).then(function (picked) {\n    return (picked && picked[0]) || null;\n  });\n}\n\n// v7.29.0: the trim was set against the PROXY's timeline, and a proxy and\n// its